---
title: "SSRM Transactions"
enterprise: true
---

This section shows how rows can be added, removed and updated using the Server-Side Transaction API.

{% note %}
Server-Side Transactions require [Row IDs](./server-side-model-configuration/#providing-row-ids) to be supplied to grid.
{% /note %}

## Transaction API

The SSRM Transaction API allows rows to be added, removed or updated in the grid:

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-api/api.json" section="serverSideRowModel" names=["applyServerSideTransaction"] /%}

{% note %}
When the server-side store has a known last row index, remove transactions only delete rows that are currently in cache. If a delete is repeated or targets a row outside the loaded range, the grid ignores it and keeps the current store size. To explicitly set the new store size, provide `rowCount` on the transaction.
{% /note %}

These operations are shown in the snippet below:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
api.applyServerSideTransaction({
    add: [
        { tradeId: 101, portfolio: 'Aggressive', product: 'Aluminium', book: 'GL-62472', current: 57969 }
    ],
    update: [
        { tradeId: 102,  portfolio: 'Aggressive', product: 'Aluminium', book: 'GL-624723', current: 58927 }
    ],
    remove: [
        { tradeId: 103 }
    ]
});
```

The following example demonstrates add / update and remove operations via the Server-Side Transaction API. Note the following:

- When clicking any of the buttons, the console logs each transaction as it is applied to the grid.
- **Add Above Selected** - adds a row above the selected row using the `addIndex` property as rows are added at the end by default.
- **Update Selected** - updates the 'current' value on the selected row.
- **Removed Selected** - removes the selected row.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Server-Side Transaction API" name="transactions-simple"  exampleHeight=615 /%}

## Row Grouping

To use transactions while using row grouping, transactions need to be applied to the specific row group. This is done by providing a `route` when applying the transaction. It is also necessary to inform the grid when group rows are updated, added or removed.

The snippet below demonstrates creating a group row transaction for rows which are the first of their group, as the leaf rows will be requested via `getRows` when the group is expanded.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
// create the group row at the root level (only if it's the first row for this group)
api.applyServerSideTransaction({
	route: [],
	add: [{ portfolio: 'Aggressive' }]
});

// otherwise, create the leaf node inside of the 'Aggressive' group
api.applyServerSideTransaction({
	route: ['Aggressive'],
	add: [row]
});
```

In the example below, note the following:

- When clicking any of the buttons, the console logs each transaction as it is applied to the grid.
- To add a new row, if the group didn't previously exist, then the route is omitted and the group row is added. If it did previously exist, then the group route is provided and the leaf node is added.
- To delete a row, if the group row would be deleted then a transaction needs to be applied to remove this group row instead of the leaf row.
- To move a row between groups, the row needs to be deleted from the old group with one transaction, and added to the new group with another.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Transactions With Groups" name="transactions-grouping"  exampleHeight=615 /%}

## Asynchronous Updates

When processing many updates rapidly, the grid will perform more smoothly if the changes are batched (as this can prevent excessive rendering). The grid can batch these changes for you without negatively impacting the user experience, and in most cases improving it.

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-api/api.json" section="serverSideRowModel" names=["applyServerSideTransactionAsync"] /%}

When using asynchronous transactions, the grid delays any transactions received within a time window (specified using `asyncTransactionWaitMillis`) and executes them together when the window has passed.

The snippet below demonstrates three asynchronous transactions applied sequentially, however because these transactions are asynchronously batched, the grid would only update the DOM once.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
// due to asynchronous batching, the following transactions are applied together preventing unnecessary DOM updates
api.applyServerSideTransactionAsync({
    add: [{ tradeId: 101, portfolio: 'Aggressive', product: 'Aluminium', book: 'GL-62472', current: 57969 }],
});

api.applyServerSideTransactionAsync({
    update: [{ tradeId: 102,  portfolio: 'Aggressive', product: 'Aluminium', book: 'GL-624723', current: 58927 }],
});

api.applyServerSideTransactionAsync({
    remove: [{ tradeId: 103 }],
});
```

In the example below, note the following:

- After starting the updates, 1 row is created, 10 rows are updated, and 1 row is deleted every 10 milliseconds.
- The transactions are batched, and only executed once every second.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Asynchronous Example" name="transactions-async"  exampleHeight=615 /%}

## Versioned Transactions

When the grid is fed by a stream of changes, such as a WebSocket, a change can arrive after the grid has loaded newer data, and changes can arrive while the rows they change are still loading. To let the grid reconcile them, set the `serverSideDataVersionMode` grid option to `'stream'`, return the version of the data with each block in `LoadSuccessParams.dataVersion`, and send the version of the data after each change as the `version` of its transaction.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
socket.onmessage = (event) => {
    const { version, trade } = JSON.parse(event.data);
    api.applyServerSideTransactionAsync({
        route: [trade.portfolio],
        version,
        update: [trade],
    });
};
```

The grid keeps the version each row was loaded or last changed with, and the newest version each level has seen:

- An update to a row with a newer version is skipped. The skipped rows are returned in the `unapplied` property of the transaction result, and the status stays `Applied`.
- The adds and removes of a transaction at or before the level's version are skipped and returned in `unapplied`, as blocks read from that version already include them. Rows read from an older version, which `'stream'` mode can cache alongside them, are loaded again at the level's version. Return `rowCount` with each block, so that these responses also correct the row count. An add or remove of the grand total row is checked against the version of the grand total instead. Transactions applied in one flush load each block again at most once.
- A newer version returned with a block, or a newer transaction applied to the level, moves the level's version forward, so later blocks of the level are requested with `request.dataVersion` set to the newest version the grid has. Read the block at that version, or at a newer one.
- A block returned with a version older than the level's version is discarded and requested again with the level's version. This includes a block that was loading when a newer transaction arrived, so the block shows the transaction's changes once it loads. A transaction does not change rows of blocks that are still loading. If a block is still older after three more requests, the grid keeps its rows and logs a warning.
- `'stream'` mode needs versions that can be ordered: numbers, or other versions with `compareServerSideDataVersions`. A change whose version cannot be compared with the row's, such as two different strings without a comparator, is applied without a version check. Transactions without a version are applied without a version check.
- In the default `'snapshot'` mode, stale changes are skipped in the same way, but the level's version does not move, so every block of a level is read from the first version returned for it and does not show changes from later transactions. Use `'stream'` mode when the level receives versioned transactions.

In the example below, note the following:

- The server changes the value of a random trade every 100 milliseconds, and sends each change with the server's new version. Each message takes up to 2 seconds to arrive, so messages for the same trade can arrive out of order.
- Each group takes 200 milliseconds to load, so changes can arrive while it is loading. The server reads each group at its latest version when it responds, so the group includes those changes.
- The **Version** column shows the version at which each trade last changed. A message older than the version shown is skipped, and the count of skipped updates is shown above the grid.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Versioned Transactions" name="transactions-versioned" exampleHeight=615 /%}

## Showcase Example

The following demonstrates a more complex example of transactions, it shows subscribing to a source of updates to provide the changes, while using dynamic row grouping, aggregation, and child counts. All of which react to the changes caused by the transactions.

In the example below, note the following:

- After starting the updates, 2 rows are created, 5 rows are updated, and 2 rows are deleted once every second.
- Groups are created or destroyed when necessary by using transactions.
- The group panel has been enabled, allowing a dynamic configuration of groups.
- The group child counts and aggregations update in sync with changes to the leaf rows.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Showcase Example" name="transactions-showcase"  exampleHeight=670 /%}

## Tree Data

Transactions are also supported when using tree data. See this documented on the [SSRM Tree Data](./server-side-model-tree-data/#transactions-with-tree-data) page.
